  "narrative": {
    "intro": "South Carolina recorded $7.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state received 14,999 awards with an average award size of $522K, and obligations fell 6.0% year over year. The Department of Energy is the dominant buyer, obligating $4.5B, followed by the Department of Defense at $1.8B. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by dollars, receiving $3.6B, and Remediation Services is the next-largest industry with $831M. A few large vendors capture a big share of the state total: SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC accounted for $3.4B and SAVANNAH RIVER MISSION COMPLETION, LLC accounted for $813M. Fiscal-year context shows a peak in FY24 at $10.6B, with obligations falling to $8.3B in FY25 and to $7.8B so far in FY26. These figures are contract obligations and the FY2026 total is partial, covering the fiscal year to date. The data highlights concentration in energy and environmental work, sizable single-vendor shares, and a smaller award count per dollar relative to some states because of high average award size.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top federal buyers in South Carolina?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy is the top buyer, obligating $4.5B in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense follows with $1.8B. Other buyers with meaningful obligations include the Department of Homeland Security at $500M and the Department of Health and Human Services at $430M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors capture the largest shares of spending?",
        "body": "Facilities Support Services is the largest industry, receiving $3.6B in FY2026 to date. Remediation Services and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction received $831M and $556M respectively. SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC leads vendors with $3.4B, and SAVANNAH RIVER MISSION COMPLETION, LLC follows with $813M."
      }
    ]
  },
